  "result": "The classic bug: JWT `exp` is in **seconds** since Unix epoch, but `Date.now()` returns **milliseconds**.\n\n```js\n// Wrong \u2014 always passes because Date.now() >> exp\nif (Date.now() > payload.exp) { ... }\n\n// Correct \u2014 divide by 1000 to convert ms \u2192 s\nif (Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000) > payload.exp) { ... }\n```\n\nSo if a token expired at `exp: 1713700000` (~2024), `Date.now()` returns something like `1713700000000` \u2014 three orders of magnitude larger \u2014 making every token appear unexpired.\n\n**Fix:**\n\n```js\nconst nowInSeconds = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);\nif (nowInSeconds > payload.exp) {\n  return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Token expired' });\n}\n```\n\n**Better alternative:** use a library like `jsonwebtoken` which handles this correctly and validates `exp` automatically when you call `jwt.verify()`. Manual expiry checks are error-prone for exactly this reason.",
